{"ts": "2026-08-04T04:36:12.054441+00:00", "level": "INFO", "logger": "rtl_debug_agent.nodes.checkpoint", "run_id": "9012cddbc616", "message": "awaiting_approval", "test": "pmu.latency_test", "file": "rtl/pmu/tpe_pmu.sv", "line": 107, "explanation": "tpe_pmu.sv's per-command latency counter (latency_ctr_q) increments via a nonblocking assignment (line 104) each cycle dispatch_active_q is set, starting at 1 on dispatch_start. When cmd_done_valid fires on the same cycle as an ongoing dispatch_active_q increment, the capture 'cmd_latency_last_q <= latency_ctr_q' at line 114 reads the pre-clock (old) value of latency_ctr_q, since both assignments are nonblocking and evaluate right-hand sides before the clock edge. This means the completion cycle itself is never counted, undercounting the true latency by exactly 1 cycle. This is explicitly documented as bug #7 in a comment directly above the buggy line. The failing test confirms this off-by-one: CMD_LATENCY_LAST=4 measured vs. want=5. The fix is to capture latency_ctr_q + 1 instead of latency_ctr_q, which correctly accounts for the completion cycle.", "confidence_score": 0.9400000000000001, "confidence_label": "high", "confidence_rationale": "model self-reported 0.90; file appeared in evidence; used graph traversal; consulted spec; read patched file directly", "patch_path": "/Users/bhavanibs/Documents/Claude/rtl_debug_agent/data/runs/9012cddbc616/patch.diff"}
